From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 01:00:00 +0000
Subject: media: cedrus: H.264 encoder: size the sub-pixel buffer by the source stride

PARA1 13:10 carries the source stride in macroblocks divided by 48, as
the vendor library writes it, and the engine lays out the sub-pixel
rows of the reconstruction by it. The buffer was sized from the picture
width, so a source whose bytesperline is padded past the next multiple
of 48 macroblocks made the engine read past the end of the buffer: on
the H616, 1280 wide with the field at 6 gives an IOMMU read fault at
the first page after the sub-pixel buffer and an encode timeout.

Size the buffer from the larger of the width and the stride.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Arun Iyengar <aiyengar1990@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_enc_h264.c b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_enc_h264.c
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_enc_h264.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_enc_h264.c
@@ -291,7 +291,8 @@
 	struct device *dev = ctx->dev->dev;
 	unsigned int width_mbs = h264_ctx->width_mbs;
 	unsigned int height_mbs = h264_ctx->height_mbs;
-	unsigned int subpix_width, subpix_height, chroma_size;
+	unsigned int stride_mbs = ctx->src_fmt.bytesperline / 16;
+	unsigned int subpix_width, subpix_height, chroma_size, subpix_mbs;
 	struct cedrus_enc_h264_rec *rec;
 
 	rec = kzalloc(sizeof(*rec), GFP_KERNEL);
@@ -309,8 +310,13 @@
 	if (!rec->rec)
 		goto error_rec;
 
-	subpix_width = ALIGN_DOWN((width_mbs + 47) * 2 / 3, 32) +
-		       ALIGN(width_mbs, 32) * 2;
+	/*
+	 * The engine lays the sub-pixel rows out by the stride it is given in
+	 * PARA1 (the source stride), which can exceed the width.
+	 */
+	subpix_mbs = max(width_mbs, stride_mbs);
+	subpix_width = ALIGN_DOWN((subpix_mbs + 47) * 2 / 3, 32) +
+		       ALIGN(subpix_mbs, 32) * 2;
 	subpix_height = (height_mbs * 16 + 72) / 8;
 	rec->subpix_size = ALIGN(subpix_width * subpix_height, SZ_4K);
 
